member.summary:
Typed messages on top of a :

var messenger = new NetClientMessenger(client, registry);
messenger.On<Chat>(chat => Console.WriteLine(chat.Text));
await messenger.SendAsync(0, new Chat { Text = "hi" });

Handlers run where the client raises DataReceived (PollEvents caller in Polled mode).

Encrypt payloads on this channel with the connection's negotiated cipher suite
(AES-256-GCM when both sides run net8.0, AES-256-CBC + HMAC-SHA256 otherwise — see
). Requires  mode ≠ None.

Relay (and hole-punching rendezvous) for games and apps hosted behind NAT. A host claims a room; clients join it by the room code; the relay forwards their packets. For UDP it also introduces client and host to each other so they can try a direct path — when both NATs allow it, the relay drops out of the data path entirely.
The relay is deliberately dumb: it forwards NetSync packets without understanding them. On connections with a key exchange those packets are authenticated (and optionally encrypted) end to end, so a relay can neither read nor forge traffic — run it anywhere, trust it with nothing but availability.
Runs TCP and UDP on one port. Host it as a small service: using var relay = new NetRelayServer(); await relay.StartAsync(7000);

A room on a : the way to reach a server behind NAT.

Host — keep accepting LAN clients directly, add the relay as extra listeners:

var room = new RelayRoom(RelayRoom.NewRoomCode()) { RelayEndPoint = relayEndPoint };
server.AddListener(TransportType.Tcp, room.Tcp, 0);
server.AddListener(TransportType.Udp, room.Udp, 0);
await server.StartAsync(7777);   // tell players room.RoomId

Client — same channels, the relay's address, the room code:

var room = new RelayRoom("K7QX2M");
config.TransportFactories[TransportType.Tcp] = room.Tcp;
config.TransportFactories[TransportType.Udp] = room.Udp;
await client.ConnectAsync("relay.example.com", 7000);

The UDP side tries a direct, hole-punched path first () and
stays on the relay when the NATs don't allow one; the stream side always goes
through the relay (TCP can't be hole-punched reliably). To take all traffic off the
relay, configure only UDP channels — ReliableOrdered UDP replaces a TCP channel — and
register only . Encryption and authentication stay end to end — the
relay only sees envelopes.

A host that loses the relay (relay restart, network outage) keeps reclaiming its room
in the background, with growing pauses up to ; clients
that were connected through the relay reconnect on their own ().
